On Sat, 17 Oct 2026 01:20:15 -0700, Pat Maddox <pat@patmaddox.com> wrote:
> I previously said it is okay to base work on b/dev, since that's my view
> of the world.

That rule came in with "[PATCH v3 1/4] claude: Permit work based on
b/dev" [1], and the lab-process-mail side with "[PATCH v2]
src/lab-process-mail: Take the base from LAB_BASE_BRANCH" [2]. Both
are on trunk.

> However, I think that's a bad idea. trunk is stable, b/dev is not. If
> new work depends on b/dev and can't be done on trunk, then it's not
> ready and needs to wait until its dependencies make it into trunk.

Agreed. I posted the change as "[PATCH 0/2] Base work on trunk, not
b/dev" [3], based on trunk. Patch 1 replaces the CLAUDE.md paragraph
that permitted b/dev-based work with your rule: base on trunk, read
b/dev only to see what is coming, and say so on the list and wait
when work needs something only b/dev has. Patch 2 says the same in
the lab-process-mail prompt. The series supersedes "[PATCH] claude:
Fix a typo in the b/dev guidance" [4], since the paragraph it fixed
is gone, and I dropped that patch from my tree.

I kept LAB_BASE_BRANCH. It defaults to trunk(), and it still serves
your own assistant if you want one that sees the tree as you do.
The other option is to remove it, along with the base-branch: logic
in the prompt. That is simpler, but you lose that view. I recommend
keeping it.

One thing for you to do: this pass still ran with "Your base is
b/dev@upstream", so the operator setting is still in place. Please
unset LAB_BASE_BRANCH for my runs. The next pass then rebases my open
work onto trunk. Any of it that conflicts, or that needs a commit
only on b/dev, I will report on its thread rather than repost.

